{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1151,1300p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"./e2e-test.sh       # End-to-end test\n```\n\nFor self-signed TLS (localhost/LAN):\n```bash\n# CLI flag\ntask-board --insecure q --format compact 'summary()'\n\n# Or environment (inherited by spawned agents)\nexport TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY=1\n\n# Or unified config file (project-local)\n# ./task-board.config.json  (NOTE: no token here — use 'task-board login')\n{\n  \"mode\":\"remote\",\n  \"local\":{\"board_dir\":\".task-board\"},\n  \"remote\":{\n    \"url\":\"https://board.local\",\n    \"board\":\"my-project\",\n    \"tls_no_verify\":true,\n    \"spawn\":{\"enabled\":true,\"max_parallel\":20}\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### User management (service mode)\n\n```bash\n# Create user + token\ntask-board admin user create alice\ntask-board admin token create alice\n\n# Grant board access\ntask-board admin access grant alice my-project editor\n\n# List users\ntask-board admin user list\n```\n\nRoles: `admin` (full access), `editor` (read/write board), `viewer` (read-only).\n\n### Makefile reference\n\nThese targets are build helpers. They are not a replacement for `./scripts/setup.sh` when you need the installed skill copy, role sync, or self-update metadata.\n\n```bash\nmake                    # install-frontend (CLI + TUI)\nmake install-frontend   # CLI + TUI — developer workstation\nmake install-backend    # CLI + server — deployment\nmake install            # all three binaries\nmake test               # run all tests\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\n```\n\n---\n---\n\n# Part 2: Planning\n\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\n\nCanonical planning API:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\n```\n\nScope forms:\n\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\n\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\n\n```\nactive:false\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\nelements:4\nmode:children\nscope:EPIC-01\nstatuses:{\"TASK-01\":\"done\",\"TASK-02\":\"development\",\"TASK-03\":\"backlog\",\"TASK-04\":\"backlog\"}\nwaves:[[\"TASK-01\",\"TASK-03\"],[\"TASK-02\"],[\"TASK-04\"]]\n```\n\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\n\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\n\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\n\n```bash\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\n```\n\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\n\n---\n\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\n\n## Assigning Agents\n\n```bash\n# Assign agent to element\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\n\n# Remove assignment\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\n```\n\n## Agent Dashboard\n\n```bash\n# Show active agents\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\n\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\n```\n\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\n\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\n\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\n   ```\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\n\n## Agent Roles\n\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\n\n### Role Registry\n\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\n\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
