{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1301,1450p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":".roles/\n├── developer/\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\n├── researcher/\n│   ├── role.md\n│   └── skills.md\n├── tester/\n│   └── role.md\n└── ...\n```\n\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: developer\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\n  - Code written per task description and AC\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\n    classes: [code]\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\n    classes: [code]\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\n---\n\n# developer\n\n## Description\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\n\n## Standing Orders\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\n\n## Deliverable\nCode + tests.\n...\n```\n\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\n\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\n\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\n```markdown\n## Required\n- project-management\n- go-tui-testing-tools\n\n## Domain\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\n- swiftui (when building UI)\n```\n\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\n\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\n\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\n\n**Available roles:**\n\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\n\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\n\nThe spawn pipeline:\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\n\n```bash\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n```\n\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\n\nFor coordinator orchestration:\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\n\n### Skill Injection\n\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\n\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\n\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\n\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\n\nKey points covered there:\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\n\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1451,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1451,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\n\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\n\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\n\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\n\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\n- task description comes from the element README\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\n\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\n\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\n\nThe prompt also includes:\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\n\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\n\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\n\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\n\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\n\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\n\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\n\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\n\n---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '600,700p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '600,700p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\n\nSpawn is task-centric:\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\n- Task description comes from the board README\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \"Fused Spawn And Wait\"\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"`; do not assume live in-session push\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\n\n### Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\n\n### Model Selection Guide\n\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\n\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\n\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\n\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\n\n```bash\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\"\n\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\n```\n\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\n\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\n\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\n\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\n\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\n\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\n\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
