{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md | sed -n '140,180p;1560,1620p'\nnl -ba /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1a52au/curator-spec-worktree/protocol/core.md | sed -n '510,545p;980,1070p'\nnl -ba .task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_core-contract-outcome.md | sed -n '55,80p'\"","aggregated_output":"   140\t- it is valid Unicode scalar text whose UTF-8 encoding is 1 through 255 bytes;\n   141\t- it does not start or end with `/`, contain `//`, or end with `.`;\n   142\t- no slash-separated component starts with `.` or ends with `.lock`;\n   143\t- it contains none of NUL, ASCII control bytes, DEL, space, `~`, `^`, `:`, `?`, `*`, `[`, or backslash;\n   144\t- it contains neither `..` nor `@{` and is not the single character `@`;\n   145\t- the exact constructed `refs/tags/<tag>` passes the protocol-defined equivalent of `git check-ref-format` without normalization.\n   146\t\n   147\tThe manager never passes an unvalidated tag as a revision expression. Network\n   148\tacquisition passes only exact `refs/tags/<tag>` or `refs/heads/<branch>` fetch\n   149\tsources; manager code performs tag peeling after object-ID recomputation. If a\n   150\tseparately specified trusted Git verifier is ever used, its only permitted\n   151\trevision spelling is `refs/tags/<tag>^{commit}` after validation and after\n   152\t`--end-of-options` where supported.\n   153\t\n   154\tOperator network substitutions use a structured ref:\n   155\t\n   156\t- `revision` is a full lowercase object ID for the effective repository object format;\n   157\t- `tag` uses the rules above and constructs `refs/tags/<value>^{commit}`;\n   158\t- `branch` applies the same grammar to `refs/heads/<value>` and constructs only that ref;\n   159\t- no other revision operators or reflog syntax are accepted.\n   160\t\n   161\t### 3.3 Lock and optional tag assertion\n   162\t\n   163\tThe full object ID is the immutable lock. The tag is only a human-facing assertion:\n   164\t\n   165\t1. The effective declared-source repository object format MUST equal `locked_commit.object_format`.\n   166\t2. The object named by `locked_commit.hex` MUST exist and be a commit.\n   167\t3. If `tag` is present on an unsubstituted declaration, the manager MUST\n   168\t   acquire exactly `refs/tags/<tag>` in fresh operation-private state, recompute\n   169\t   and peel its lightweight or annotated tag chain under\n   170\t   `--no-replace-objects`, and require the terminal commit to equal\n   171\t   `locked_commit.hex`. Mismatch is `build_repository_ref_moved`; a missing or\n   172\t   inaccessible exact ref is `build_repository_source_unavailable`.\n   173\t4. Install, repair, status, and audit never rewrite a lock. An authoring command may propose a manifest change, but it is outside installation.\n   174\t5. A declaration without `tag` acquires only the full locked object ID. A\n   175\t   declaration with `tag` acquires only the exact tag ref; server permission to\n   176\t   fetch a direct object ID is irrelevant and MUST NOT change this choice.\n   177\t   Failure never permits a branch, all-tags, alternate-ref, or direct-object\n   178\t   fallback.\n   179\t\n   180\t## 4. Repository descriptor and output boundary\n  1560\t\n  1561\tFor an unsubstituted external build, `declared_tag` may be written only from a\n  1562\treceipt whose producing operation completed the exact-tag/locked-commit check.\n  1563\tMarker parsing does not turn that field into independent provenance, and\n  1564\tread-only status does not contact the remote to re-test later tag movement.\n  1565\t\n  1566\t### 9.1 Snapshot and artifact keys\n  1567\t\n  1568\t- Protected external snapshot key: effective identity kind/value, effective object format, full effective commit, and external build-source digest.\n  1569\t- Artifact key: SHA-256 of the entire receipt input, including both declared and effective states, command key, descriptor target, build root/source directory, target, toolchain, and policy.\n  1570\t- Snapshot bytes may be deduplicated only when the complete snapshot key matches. Audit decisions remain subject-specific.\n  1571\t- Artifacts may be reused only when the complete receipt input and protected entry validate. Different declarations, substitutions, commands, targets, toolchains, or policy revisions do not alias.\n  1572\t- Physical object, snapshot, artifact, receipt, lock, and quarantine paths remain implementation-specific.\n  1573\t\n  1574\t### 9.2 Read-only status\n  1575\t\n  1576\tStatus MUST NOT fetch, repair, quarantine, alter permissions, refresh a snapshot, invoke a compiler, or execute an artifact.\n  1577\t\n  1578\tIt:\n  1579\t\n  1580\t1. derives the current effective plan from manifest and operator dev configuration;\n  1581\t2. validates marker v3 and substitution equality;\n  1582\t3. revalidates every referenced protected skill/external snapshot and artifact boundary;\n  1583\t4. recomputes available build-source identities, receipt inputs/keys, receipt hashes, and artifact relationships;\n  1584\t5. structurally validates manager-owned shims and PATH exposure.\n  1585\t\n  1586\tOutcomes:\n  1587\t\n  1588\t- an identity, commit, digest, target, receipt, artifact, shim, or substitution mismatch is non-current;\n  1589\t- an untrusted or corrupt snapshot/artifact boundary is non-current;\n  1590\t- a missing required snapshot is non-current when the marker claims it should exist;\n  1591\t- when existing profile semantics distinguish unavailable evidence from drift, a source that cannot be proved read-only may be reported `unknown`, but MUST NOT be reported current and `status --check` MUST return non-zero;\n  1592\t- status never contacts the remote merely to test availability or tag movement.\n  1593\t\n  1594\t### 9.3 Repair\n  1595\t\n  1596\tRepair resolves the current effective operator substitution if present, otherwise the declared locked source. It reacquires into operation-private state, repeats object proof, validation, identity, and audit, and rebuilds or reuses only after protected admission. It never adopts an untrusted snapshot or artifact by changing permissions.\n  1597\t\n  1598\tFor an unsubstituted declaration containing `tag`, repair always reacquires by\n  1599\tthe exact-tag-only flow and rechecks terminal equality with the immutable lock.\n  1600\tIt cannot use a successful direct-object fetch or an old protected snapshot as\n  1601\ta substitute for that operation's tag assertion.\n  1602\t\n  1603\tIf repair cannot obtain the exact source, it fails before installation mutation. Publication, marker/shim changes, rollback, and consumer update use the existing manager-home mutation lock and journal.\n  1604\t\n  1605\t### 9.4 Garbage collection roots\n  1606\t\n  1607\tGC revalidates cache boundaries and, under the manager-home mutation lock:\n  1608\t\n  1609\t- valid marker v3 files mark every referenced local skill raw snapshot, external frozen snapshot key, receipt/artifact key, and manager-generated shim relation;\n  1610\t- in-flight transaction journals mark every staged/published snapshot and artifact needed for commit or rollback;\n  1611\t- valid marker v1/v2 behavior remains unchanged;\n  1612\t- receipt content alone is never a live root;\n  1613\t- unreadable markers/journals or unprovable cache boundaries retain uncertain entries conservatively;\n  1614\t- GC never executes or adopts source or artifact bytes.\n  1615\t\n  1616\t## 10. Deterministic build, install, PATH, and rollback\n  1617\t\n  1618\t`go-repository-v1` reuses the accepted `go-v1` trusted Go session, environment, full dependency checks, directive/native-input rejection, fixed `go list`/`go build` vectors, internal-link policy, artifact checks, and no-execution rule. Its source acquisition and receipt semantics are distinct, so the driver ID is distinct.\n  1619\t\n  1620\tNormative order:\n   510\tno-`.`/no-`..` component rules. A quote, whitespace, shell metacharacter,\n   511\tescape, or non-ASCII byte MUST be rejected before Git or SSH starts. HTTPS\n   512\tretains the Unicode path grammar above.\n   513\t\n   514\tA manifest tag is the name below `refs/tags/` and MUST:\n   515\t\n   516\t1. encode to 1 through 255 UTF-8 bytes of valid Unicode scalar text;\n   517\t2. neither start nor end with `/`, contain `//`, nor end with `.`;\n   518\t3. have no slash component starting with `.` or ending with `.lock`;\n   519\t4. contain none of NUL, ASCII control bytes, DEL, space, `~`, `^`, `:`, `?`,\n   520\t   `*`, `[`, or backslash;\n   521\t5. contain neither `..` nor `@{` and not equal the single character `@`; and\n   522\t6. make the exact constructed `refs/tags/<tag>` pass the protocol equivalent\n   523\t   of `git check-ref-format` without normalization.\n   524\t\n   525\tA manager MUST NOT pass a tag as an unqualified revision expression. An\n   526\toperator network substitution MUST use exactly one structured ref:\n   527\t\n   528\t- `revision` is a full lowercase object ID for the declared object format;\n   529\t- `tag` uses the grammar above and selects only `refs/tags/<value>`; or\n   530\t- `branch` uses the same safe-name grammar and selects only\n   531\t  `refs/heads/<value>`.\n   532\t\n   533\tNo other revision, peel, reflog, range, or normalization syntax is admitted.\n   534\t\n   535\tThe declared full object ID is immutable. Its object format MUST equal the\n   536\teffective repository format and the named object MUST be a commit. An\n   537\tunsubstituted declaration without `tag` MUST acquire only\n   538\t`<full-locked-oid>:refs/curator/locked`. An unsubstituted declaration with\n   539\t`tag` MUST instead acquire only\n   540\t`refs/tags/<tag>:refs/curator/tag` in fresh operation-private state, recompute\n   541\tand peel the exact lightweight or annotated-tag chain, and require its terminal\n   542\tcommit to equal the full lock. Server policy permitting or denying direct\n   543\tobject-ID wants MUST NOT alter the tagged flow.\n   544\t\n   545\tThe outer annotated tag object MUST name the requested tag. Every annotated\n   980\tvisible or runtime-copied external source, untrusted boundary, corrupt\n   981\treceipt/artifact, wrong target or toolchain, or mismatched source identity\n   982\tmakes the installation non-current. When existing profile semantics\n   983\tdistinguish unavailable evidence from drift, a result MAY be `unknown`, but it\n   984\tMUST NOT be current and a checking status command MUST return non-zero.\n   985\t\n   986\tRead-only status MUST NOT contact an external remote merely to retest tag\n   987\tavailability or movement. It MUST NOT fetch, repair, quarantine, alter\n   988\tpermissions, refresh a snapshot, invoke a compiler or signer, or execute an\n   989\tartifact. Repair MUST reacquire, validate, and audit the exact effective source\n   990\tand MUST rebuild into protected state rather than adopt candidate bytes. For an\n   991\tunsubstituted tagged source, repair MUST use only the exact-tag path and MUST\n   992\treprove terminal equality with the immutable lock; an old snapshot or direct-\n   993\tobject fetch MUST NOT substitute for that assertion.\n   994\t\n   995\tUnsupported or unreadable markers are not current. A moved tag is a warning,\n   996\tor an error under strict-tag policy.\n   997\t\n   998\tThe marker and its referenced receipt hashes are not signatures and MUST NOT be\n   999\tused as authorization tokens or provenance proof. Registry attestations are\n  1000\treverified from signed records when fresh trust is required.\n  1001\t\n  1002\t## 11. Adapter ledger\n  1003\t\n  1004\tEvery managed adapter root contains `.csk-managed.json` conforming to\n  1005\t`adapter-ledger-v1.schema.json`. `entries` is a sorted unique list of skill\n  1006\tnames owned by the manager in that adapter root. A manager MUST remove only\n  1007\tentries in its preceding ledger and MUST fail rather than overwrite an\n  1008\tunmanaged conflicting entry.\n  1009\t\n  1010\t## 12. External build publication and closed extensions\n  1011\t\n  1012\t### 12.1 Publication, shims, and rollback\n  1013\t\n  1014\tAll `go-repository-v1` compilation MUST occur in operation-private staging\n  1015\tbefore installation mutation. The manager MUST validate and hash the sole\n  1016\tmanager-derived artifact, generate canonical receipt and marker bytes, and\n  1017\tpublish immutable snapshots and artifacts only under the manager-home mutation\n  1018\tlock. The consumer ledger MUST commit last. A failure after publication begins\n  1019\tMUST roll committed targets back in reverse order while retaining that lock.\n  1020\tPre-publication source, object, descriptor, audit, cache, or compiler failures\n  1021\tMUST leave live installation state unchanged.\n  1022\t\n  1023\tOne manifest command key MAY select the same descriptor target as another\n  1024\tcommand key, but each command MUST derive its own artifact name, cache input,\n  1025\treceipt, and shim. The repository MUST NOT request aliases, sidecars,\n  1026\tpost-build copies, or secondary PATH entries. A build shim MUST be manager-\n  1027\towned and point exactly to the marker-selected protected artifact. It MUST NOT\n  1028\tpoint into a Git object database, frozen source snapshot, checkout, staging\n  1029\tdirectory, or commit-keyed script runtime. Structural validation MUST NOT use a\n  1030\tshell, executable lookup, or execution of the built output.\n  1031\t\n  1032\t### 12.2 Operator credentials and signing\n  1033\t\n  1034\tGit/SSH/HTTPS executables, credentials, host-verification state, proxy policy,\n  1035\ttimeouts, and authentication mode are operator-owned. They MUST NOT be\n  1036\tselected by a manifest, descriptor, repository, substitution, compiler\n  1037\tenvironment, receipt trust field, or marker. Source credentials MUST NOT enter\n  1038\tthe compiler environment.\n  1039\t\n  1040\t`go-repository-v1` revision 1 performs no manager post-signing, timestamping,\n  1041\tor notarization. Signing identities and notarization credentials are operator\n  1042\tor release-pipeline secrets and MUST NOT appear in a manifest, descriptor,\n  1043\trepository, receipt trust field, or marker. A platform policy requiring local\n  1044\tsigning MUST reject the source-built artifact until a separately versioned and\n  1045\treviewed signer profile defines the fixed signer identity, executable, argv,\n  1046\tentitlements/options, process graph, network policy, signed-byte and cache\n  1047\tidentity, protected publication, and rollback rules.\n  1048\t\n  1049\t### 12.3 Future closed-driver admission\n  1050\t\n  1051\tThe external Git envelope MAY be reused, but compiler semantics MUST NOT become\n  1052\tgeneric. Every future language driver requires a new closed identifier and an\n  1053\tindependent protocol and security review. Its normative contract MUST define:\n  1054\t\n  1055\t- complete compiler-visible input and dependency containment;\n  1056\t- trusted toolchain, sysroot, SDK, and runtime identity;\n  1057\t- fixed process graph, environment, arguments, flags, target, output, and\n  1058\t  signer boundary;\n  1059\t- offline dependency, source identity, link, and native-library policy;\n  1060\t- rejection of hooks, plugins, macros, generators, annotation processors,\n  1061\t  build tasks, recipes, response files, package linkers, and produced-program\n  1062\t  execution; and\n  1063\t- receipt/marker/cache identity, audit-before-build ordering, dry-run,\n  1064\t  publication, rollback/recovery, status, repair, garbage collection, and\n  1065\t  platform conformance vectors.\n  1066\t\n  1067\tA manager MUST NOT admit a future driver by widening `go-v1` or\n  1068\t`go-repository-v1`, accepting an unknown driver, or providing a generic build\n  1069\tor package-manager fallback.\n    55\t| 6.6.3 Git LFS rejection | Core 6.5 pins `git-lfs-pointer-parser-v3.7.1` and stable LFS failure; Security prohibits hydration | Exact parser-family algorithm/fixtures belong to TASK-260728-wy3dsw and TASK-260728-3b8qym |\n    56\t| 7. Snapshot identity/audit equivalence | Core 6.5, 8.1, 9.3; Security “Descriptor, output, and audit isolation”; Decision 0005 “Raw snapshot and audit boundary” | Registry/profile CLI presentation remains TASK-260728-wy3dsw |\n    57\t| 8.1 Unsubstituted receipt v2 | Core 9.2; Core 6.3/6.4; Decision 0005 “Receipt, marker, status, and lifecycle” | Receipt-v2 schema/canonical cases remain TASK-260728-17sclp |\n    58\t| 8.2 Substituted receipt v2 | Core 6.4 and 9.2 | Receipt-v2 schema/cases remain TASK-260728-17sclp |\n    59\t| 8.3 Mixed commands | Core 4.2, 9.1/9.2, 10; Decision 0005 compatibility/lifecycle | Marker-v3 conditional branches remain TASK-260728-17sclp |\n    60\t| 9. Marker/status/repair/dedup/GC | Core 10 and 12.1; Decision 0005 lifecycle | Full manager status/repair/dedup/GC state machine and diagnostics belong to TASK-260728-wy3dsw; marker schema to TASK-260728-17sclp |\n    61\t| 9.1 Snapshot/artifact keys | Core 9.2/9.3 and 10 | Physical paths remain intentionally implementation-specific |\n    62\t| 9.2 Read-only status | Core 10; Security “Offline, status, credential, and signing ownership” | CLI output contract belongs to TASK-260728-wy3dsw |\n    63\t| 9.3 Repair | Core 10 exact-tag-only repair and no-adoption rule | Transaction details belong to TASK-260728-wy3dsw |\n    64\t| 9.4 GC roots | Core 10/12 protected marker relationships | Complete GC root walk belongs to TASK-260728-wy3dsw |\n    65\t| 10. Deterministic build/install/PATH/rollback | Core 4.2.1 and 12.1; Security descriptor/output isolation; Decision 0005 lifecycle | Exact lifecycle state machine belongs to TASK-260728-wy3dsw |\n    66\t| 11. Signing/notarization | Core 12.2; Security “Offline, status, credential, and signing ownership”; Decision 0005 credential/signing ownership | Future signer and release-pipeline implementation are out of rc.5 scope |\n    67\t| 12. Future closed drivers | Core 12.3; Security “Closed future-driver admission”; Decision 0005 future-driver ownership | No future driver admitted by this task |\n    68\t| 13. Threat model | Security “External build repository boundary”; Decision 0005 “Security impact” | Platform-native enforcement evidence remains conformance/implementation work |\n    69\t| 14. Board impact | Core 1 and Decision 0005 freeze rc.4; this outcome records task ownership | Curator/cocoaskills/interoperability implementation tasks are explicitly out of curator-spec core scope |\n    70\t| 15. Rejected alternatives | Decision 0005 “Rejected alternatives”; Core and Security MUST NOT rules | None |\n    71\t| 16. Primary-source register | Binding architecture digest retained in Decision 0005 and this outcome | Source bibliography is architecture evidence; exact operational citations may be carried into manager-profile/conformance docs |\n    72\t| 17. Validation/review checks | This outcome’s validation evidence and ownership map | Schema/example/vector gates belong to TASK-260728-17sclp and TASK-260728-3b8qym |\n    73\t| 17.1 Producer evidence | Binding digest verification and gates below | Historical architecture smoke details are evidence, not copied into normative prose |\n    74\t\n    75\t## Recorded exclusions\n    76\t\n    77\t- No JSON Schema, generated case, vector, release metadata, CLI, manager\n    78\t  profile, Curator, or cocoaskills file was changed.\n    79\t- Exact Git init/fetch/SSH/cat-file argv, byte-level local Git parsers, full\n    80\t  status/repair/GC CLI behavior, and stable diagnostic rendering are reserved\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
